= Software =

== Overview ==
'''Software''' is the non-physical computational artifact layer that directs computing systems to perform useful work. It includes [[Program (computing)|programs]] and, depending on context, associated libraries, configuration, data definitions, documentation, models, interfaces, deployment artifacts, and other information required for operation or maintenance.

Software is usually contrasted with '''hardware''', the physical components of a computing system. The boundary is useful but not always sharp: firmware, programmable hardware, microcode, and embedded systems combine physical and logical layers closely.

== Software and program ==
A program is an executable or interpretable expression of behavior. Software is the broader engineered artifact or system.

A small utility may consist largely of one program. A browser, operating system, cloud service, or database can contain many programs plus libraries, configuration, schemas, assets, interfaces, tests, build logic, deployment definitions, and external dependencies.

== What belongs to software? ==
Standards and engineering practice do not always draw the same boundary.

Depending on purpose, "software" may include:

* source and executable programs;
* libraries and frameworks;
* configuration;
* schemas and data definitions;
* installation/deployment artifacts;
* tests;
* build definitions;
* operational scripts;
* documentation;
* generated artifacts.

The [[System boundary|system boundary]] should therefore be stated when a claim depends on exactly what is included.

== Major forms ==
Common categories include:

'''System software''' — foundational services for other software, such as operating systems and runtimes.

'''Application software''' — software intended to perform user or organizational tasks.

'''Embedded software''' — software running within a larger physical device or machine.

'''Libraries and frameworks''' — reusable software components used to construct other systems.

'''Services''' — network-accessible software components, often composed into distributed systems.

These categories overlap and should not be treated as mutually exclusive natural kinds.

== Software engineering ==
Software engineering applies systematic engineering practices to software development, operation, maintenance, and evolution.

Important concerns include:

* requirements;
* architecture and design;
* implementation;
* verification and testing;
* configuration and [[Version control|version control]];
* security;
* reliability and safety;
* performance;
* usability;
* deployment and operations;
* maintenance and evolution.

The SWEBOK Guide organizes generally accepted software-engineering knowledge into a set of knowledge areas and is useful as a field map, not as the sole definition of software.

== Software complexity ==
Software can embody highly complex conceptual structures even when its physical storage is inexpensive to reproduce.

Brooks's "No Silver Bullet" distinguished essential complexity arising from the conceptual problem from accidental complexity introduced by particular tools or representations. The distinction remains influential even though specific technologies continue to change.

== Dependencies and environment ==
Modern software is layered. A program may depend on:

* libraries;
* package ecosystems;
* operating systems;
* runtimes;
* network protocols;
* databases;
* containers or virtual machines;
* external services;
* hardware capabilities.

This reuse makes complex systems feasible but also creates supply-chain, compatibility, security, and reproducibility risks.

== Lifecycle and evolution ==
Software is rarely static. It is revised as requirements, environments, dependencies, threats, and users change.

[[Version control]] records revision history, but maintenance also requires issue tracking, testing, release management, migration, observability, documentation, and configuration management.

== Software as part of a larger system ==
A software product often participates in a sociotechnical or cyber-physical system that includes people, organizations, devices, policies, networks, and external services.

Calling all of those surrounding elements "software" can hide important boundaries. Conversely, treating software as only source code can ignore the dependencies and operational information required for real behavior.

== Epistemic notes ==
"Software" is established terminology, but its exact extension is contextual. BITwiki should explicitly state the relevant artifact set when making claims about provenance, security, reproducibility, licensing, or failure.

